message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'Post-Competence: Verification Precision Caps the Reliability Compute Buys'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/post-competence.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-post-competence
abstract: A person who cannot do a task can still invoke a machine system many times, and the claim that power therefore equals compute holds only if they can tell good outputs from bad. This paper models an invoker who resamples a system with per-attempt success probability $p$ and accepts outputs with a check of sensitivity $s$ and false-acceptance rate $f$. Accepting the first output that passes converges to the check's precision, $sp/[sp+f(1-p)]$, a known limit for verifier-based selection. A target reliability is reachable with finite compute exactly when $s/f$ exceeds the target's odds divided by the generator's odds. Across tasks, resampling falls short of a check's pooled precision by the covariance between per-task acceptance and precision, divided by mean acceptance; with a common check the ceiling lies between single-attempt accuracy and the pooled formula. Re-checking a candidate changes nothing when verdicts are fixed by the candidate. On 40 public olympiad problems a small current model was right on 88% of attempts. Its own verdicts accepted 41% of wrong attempts, mostly its repeated errors, and capped resampling at 92%, against 94% with a stronger model as checker and 95% with a perfect check. On 25 harder problems (58% right), its self-check raised reliability only to 60%, while the stronger checker reached the 72% a perfect check allows. By hand classification, at least 30% and 67% of the stronger model's verdicts invoked a remembered answer, so its advantage partly reflects memory. With these samples most differences between checks remain unresolved.
